# Richard Stallman

Richard Matthew Stallman (born 1953), often known by his initials RMS, is an American computer programmer and software freedom activist who founded the free software movement. In 1983, he launched the GNU Project with the goal of creating a completely free, Unix-like operating system, a reaction against the growing commercialization and proprietary enclosure of software code. To support this endeavor, Stallman founded the Free Software Foundation (FSF) in 1985 and pioneered the legal concept of "copyleft," formalizing it through the widely utilized GNU General Public License (GPL). This licensing mechanism fundamentally transformed software engineering by ensuring that software and its derivatives remain free for all users to run, study, modify, and distribute. As a highly accomplished programmer, Stallman personally developed several foundational tools of the GNU system, most notably the GNU Emacs text editor, the GNU Compiler Collection (GCC), and the GNU Debugger (GDB). His philosophical framework emphasizes the moral and ethical imperative of software freedom, arguing that proprietary software subjugates users and divides communities. Although his strict ideological stances and controversial public statements have frequently drawn criticism, leading to temporary resignations from prominent institutional roles, Stallman's technical and legal frameworks laid the indispensable groundwork for the modern open-source and free software ecosystems, including the GNU/Linux operating system.

## Early Life and Education

Richard Matthew Stallman was born on March 16, 1953, in New York City. He demonstrated a strong aptitude for mathematics and physics from a young age. While attending high school, he spent summers at the IBM New York Scientific Center, where he wrote his first computer programs.

Stallman enrolled at Harvard University in 1970, where he distinguished himself in advanced mathematics and physics courses. During his freshman year, he became affiliated with the Artificial Intelligence Laboratory at the Massachusetts Institute of Technology (MIT). He soon became a prominent programmer within MIT's hacker community, a culture characterized by its ethos of sharing code and resisting bureaucratic limitations on computer access. Stallman graduated from Harvard in 1974 with a bachelor's degree in physics *magna cum laude* and subsequently enrolled as a graduate student in physics at MIT. However, he eventually abandoned his graduate studies to focus entirely on programming at the MIT AI Lab.

## The Decline of Hacker Culture and the GNU Project

During the late 1970s and early 1980s, the collaborative hacker culture at MIT began to fragment. Software companies started hiring top programmers from the AI Lab and began enforcing non-disclosure agreements (NDAs) to protect proprietary source code.

A pivotal event in Stallman's ideological development occurred when the AI Lab received a new Xerox 9700 laser printer. The printer frequently jammed, and because Xerox refused to provide the source code for the printer's software, Stallman was unable to implement a fix that would alert users to paper jams—a modification he had successfully programmed on previous printers. Viewing this withholding of code as fundamentally anti-social and restrictive, Stallman became determined to create an ecosystem where software could be freely shared.

In September 1983, Stallman announced the GNU Project (a recursive acronym for "GNU's Not Unix") on various ARPANET mailing lists. His objective was to write a complete, Unix-compatible operating system comprising exclusively free software, ensuring that users would never be subjected to proprietary restrictions.

## The Free Software Foundation and Copyleft

To provide a legal and financial infrastructure for the GNU Project, Stallman founded the Free Software Foundation (FSF) in 1985, a non-profit organization dedicated to promoting the universal right to create, distribute, and modify computer programs.

### The Four Essential Freedoms

Through the FSF, Stallman articulated the formal definition of "free software." He clarified that "free" referred to liberty, not price, famously summarizing the concept as "free as in free speech, not as in free beer." The philosophy is grounded in four essential freedoms:

1. The freedom to run the program as you wish, for any purpose.
2. The freedom to study how the program works, and change it so it does your computing as you wish.
3. The freedom to redistribute copies so you can help others.
4. The freedom to distribute copies of your modified versions to others.

### Copyleft and the GNU GPL

To ensure that GNU software remained free and could not be co-opted into proprietary products, Stallman invented the concept of "copyleft." In contrast to copyright, which restricts the distribution and modification of works, copyleft uses copyright law to guarantee those rights, stipulating that any modified versions of the software must also be distributed under the same free terms.

In 1989, Stallman formalized this concept by publishing the GNU General Public License (GPL). The GPL became one of the most widely used software licenses in the world, serving as the legal foundation for thousands of collaborative software projects.

## Technical Contributions and GNU/Linux

As a programmer, Stallman authored several complex pieces of software that were crucial to the realization of the GNU operating system. In 1985, he created GNU Emacs, a highly extensible and customizable text editor that remains intensely popular among programmers. In 1987, he released the GNU Compiler Collection (GCC), a critical infrastructure tool used to compile source code into executable machine code across various hardware architectures.

By the early 1990s, the GNU project had completed almost all the essential components of a modern operating system—including compilers, debuggers, text editors, and a graphical windowing system—except for the kernel (the core component that allocates hardware resources). When a Finnish student named Linus Torvalds released the Linux kernel under the GNU GPL in 1992, the combination of GNU software and the Linux kernel resulted in a fully functional, completely free operating system, widely referred to today as GNU/Linux.

## Activism and Ideological Stances

Stallman has spent the latter half of his career traveling globally as an activist for software freedom. He maintains a strict ideological stance, vehemently opposing Digital Rights Management (DRM)—which he terms "Digital Restrictions Management"—software patents, and proprietary software, which he considers malware due to its subjugation of the user.

Stallman also explicitly rejects the term "open source," which was coined in 1998 by individuals seeking to market free software to the corporate world without its associated moral philosophy. Stallman argues that "open source" is a development methodology focused on practical benefits, whereas "free software" is a social movement rooted in ethical imperatives.

## Controversies and Resignations

Stallman's uncompromising communication style and strict adherence to his principles have occasionally alienated peers within the technology sector.

In September 2019, Stallman resigned from his position at MIT's Computer Science and Artificial Intelligence Laboratory (CSAIL) and stepped down as president and board member of the Free Software Foundation. These resignations followed intense public backlash over comments he made on an internal MIT mailing list regarding the victims of Jeffrey Epstein and the late AI pioneer Marvin Minsky. In March 2021, the FSF board controversially reinstated Stallman, leading to a schism within the free software community, with several prominent organizations pulling funding and developers signing competing open letters demanding his removal or supporting his reinstatement.

